  "id": "t01-004",
  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "C# value types reference types copying identity and equality",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "A value-type variable contains its value; assigning it normally copies that value. A reference-type variable contains a reference; copying the variable copies the reference, so two variables can refer to the same object.",
    "The distinction is about language semantics, not a promise that every value lives on the stack or every object on the heap. Storage location is affected by context, optimization, boxing, and implementation.",
    "Equality is a separate design question from copying. The == operator, Equals, ReferenceEquals, and EqualityComparer<T>.Default can express different relationships; default behavior differs across built-in types, structs, classes, and records.",
    "Mutability makes aliasing visible: changing a shared object can affect every reference to it, while mutating a local copy of a value type doesn't mutate the original variable. A struct can still contain references to shared objects.",
    "Choose value types for small values with value semantics and reference types for identity, inheritance, or shared mutable state. Size, copying cost, lifetime, and API semantics matter more than simplistic stack-versus-heap rules."
  ],
